= Module 1: The Historical Crucible & Genesis of the Constituent Assembly

== 1.1 The Tumultuous Context of Framing (1946–1947)
The Indian Constitution was drafted against a background of catastrophic upheaval. Between 1946 and 1949, the subcontinent was convulsed by the violence of Partition, mass displacement of millions of Hindu, Muslim, and Sikh refugees, and the acute challenge of integrating *565 princely states* into a unified federal polity. 

The Constituent Assembly was convened in New Delhi under the provisions of the *Cabinet Mission Plan of 1946*. It met for the first time on *9 December 1946*. Following the Mountbatten Plan (3 June 1947) and Partition, the Muslim League boycotted the Assembly, and its membership was reconstituted from the original 389 down to *299 members*.

- *Mode of Election:* The members were not elected by universal adult franchise. Instead, they were *indirectly elected* by the provincial legislative assemblies (elected in early 1946) through proportional representation with the single transferable vote. Representatives of the princely states were chosen through negotiation and nomination.
- *Party Composition:* The Indian National Congress occupied *82% of the seats* in the reconstituted Assembly. However, the Congress was far from monolithic; it was a broad, pluralistic umbrella organization comprising socialists, free-market capitalists, agrarian traditionalists, secular modernizers, and devout Gandhians.

#exam-pointer(title: "CUET High-Yield: Representation in the Assembly")[
  - *Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha:* The oldest member, elected as the temporary / interim Chairman on 9 December 1946 following the French practice.
  - *Dr. Rajendra Prasad:* Elected as the permanent President of the Constituent Assembly on 11 December 1946.
  - *H.C. Mookherjee & V.T. Krishnamachari:* Elected as the two Vice-Presidents of the Assembly.
]

== 1.2 The Congress Triumvirate & Drafting Leadership
Six individuals played dominant roles in shaping the constitutional deliberations:

1. *Jawaharlal Nehru:* Formulated the overarching democratic and socialist vision; moved the landmark *Objectives Resolution* (13 December 1946); chaired the Union Powers Committee, Union Constitution Committee, and States Committee.
2. *Vallabhbhai Patel:* Deputy Prime Minister and Home Minister; master of political realism and consensus; chaired the Advisory Committee on Fundamental Rights, Minorities, and Tribal Areas, and the Provincial Constitution Committee; secured the integration of princely states.
3. *Dr. Rajendra Prasad:* As Assembly President, steered debates with non-partisan equanimity, ensuring that opposing perspectives received fair hearings.
4. *Dr. B.R. Ambedkar:* Appointed on 29 August 1947 as the *Chairman of the 7-member Drafting Committee*. As Law Minister, he steered the draft clause by clause, infusing the text with constitutional morality and protections for oppressed classes.
5. *Sir Benegal Narsing Rau (B.N. Rau):* *Constitutional Advisor* to the Assembly. Prepared the initial draft after an exhaustive comparative study of the constitutions of the USA, UK, Ireland, Canada, and Australia.
6. *Surendra Nath Mukherjee (S.N. Mukherjee):* *Chief Draftsman* of the Assembly. Praised by Ambedkar for his extraordinary legal skill in translating complex political compromises into precise, unambiguous statutory prose.

