= Module 2: The Objectives Resolution & Democratic Foundations

== 2.1 Nehru's Objectives Resolution (13 December 1946)
On *13 December 1946*, Jawaharlal Nehru moved the historic *Objectives Resolution*, which outlined the philosophical ideals and structural architecture of the future republic. It was unanimously adopted on *22 January 1947* and later inspired the *Preamble* of the Constitution.

#quote(attribution: [Jawaharlal Nehru, Moving the Objectives Resolution, 13 December 1946])[
  "We say that it is our firm and solemn resolve to have an Independent Sovereign Republic... There is no doubt that only a democratic constitution would be acceptable here... We are not going just to copy, I hope, a certain democratic procedure or an institution of a so-called democratic country."
]

*Core Principles of the Resolution:*
- *Sovereignty:* India was declared an Independent Sovereign Republic whose power and authority derive entirely from the sovereign people.
- *Justice & Equality:* Guarantees of social, economic, and political justice; equality of status, opportunity, and before the law.
- *Safeguards for the Marginalized:* Adequate safeguards promised for minorities, backward and tribal areas, and depressed classes.
- *Global Role:* Commitment to international peace and the welfare of mankind.

== 2.2 Universal Adult Franchise: The Bold Leap of Faith
One of the most revolutionary decisions of the Constituent Assembly was the unconditional adoption of *Universal Adult Franchise* (Article 326). 

While Western democracies (such as Britain, France, and the United States) had granted voting rights slowly over centuries—initially restricting franchise to property-owning males and only gradually enfranchising the working class and women—India granted the vote simultaneously to every adult citizen aged 21 and above (reduced to 18 by the 61st Amendment in 1988), regardless of gender, caste, religion, wealth, or literacy. In a country where over 85% of the population was illiterate and impoverished, this was an unprecedented democratic leap.

