= Module 4: Federal Architecture: Central Power vs. Provincial Autonomy

== 4.1 The Impetus for a Strong Center
The partition massacres, the refugee crisis, the threat of balkanization from recalcitrant princely rulers (such as Hyderabad, Junagadh, and Kashmir), and food shortages convinced leaders like *Jawaharlal Nehru*, *Dr. B.R. Ambedkar*, and *N. Gopalaswami Ayyangar* that only a centralized, formidable Union could preserve India's sovereignty.

Nehru declared that in the post-Partition context, a weak central government would be catastrophic: *"Now that Partition is a settled fact, it would be injurious to the interests of the country to provide for a weak central authority which would be incapable of ensuring peace, of coordinating vital matters of common concern and of speaking effectively for the whole country in the international sphere."*

== 4.2 The Critique of Over-Centralization
*K. Santhanam* (Madras) led the intellectual opposition against excessive centralization:
- He argued that an overburdened Center would inevitably falter: *"A strong Centre should not be confused with a Center that possesses all powers."*
- Santhanam pointed out that stripping provinces of fiscal resources would reduce them to administrative beggars: *"The provinces will have to beg the Centre for money to repair a school or dig a village well... If you do this, provincial autonomy will be a complete farce."*
- He warned that over-centralization would provoke regional rebellions against New Delhi.

== 4.3 The Three Legislative Lists & Unitary Tilt
The Assembly adopted a compromise that created a federal system with a distinct *unitary bias*:

