= Module 5: The Language Controversy & The Munshi-Ayyangar Formula

== 5.1 The Clash of Linguistic Visions
Language proved to be the most explosive and emotionally charged issue in the Constituent Assembly:

- *The Gandhian Ideal of Hindustani:* Mahatma Gandhi had consistently championed *Hindustani*—a composite blend of Hindi and Urdu, spoken across North India and written in both Devanagari and Persian scripts—as the ideal national language because it reflected composite cultural synthesis and bridged communal divides.
- *The Aggressive Hindi Demand:* Following Partition, Hindu traditionalists rejected Hindustani as tainted by Islamic influence and demanded that Sanskritised Hindi written in Devanagari script be declared the sole *National Language*.
  - *R.V. Dhulekar* (United Provinces) delivered an aggressive speech on 12 September 1947:
  #quote(attribution: [R.V. Dhulekar in the Constituent Assembly, 1947])[
    "People who are present in this House to fashion a constitution for India and do not know Hindustani are not worthy to be members of this Assembly. The sooner they leave this House, the better."
  ]
- *The Southern and Non-Hindi Resistance:* Members from non-Hindi-speaking regions responded with deep apprehension:
  - *Shrimati G. Durgabai* (Madras) warned that Hindi chauvinism was eroding the goodwill generated during the freedom movement: *"The aggressive advocacy of Hindi is causing intense bitterness in the South. You are isolating yourselves."*
  - *T.A. Ramalingam Chettiar* (Madras) warned that forcing Hindi would lead to regional disintegration: *"If you force this upon an unwilling people, it will lead to results which nobody will be happy about."*

== 5.2 The Munshi-Ayyangar Compromise Formula
To prevent the Assembly from fracturing along linguistic lines, a compromise formula was drafted by *K.M. Munshi* and *N. Gopalaswami Ayyangar*:

1. *Official Language vs. National Language:* Hindi in Devanagari script was declared the *Official Language* of the Union (*Article 343*). The Assembly deliberately avoided using the title "National Language" to respect India's multilingual diversity.
2. *Transition Period for English:* For an initial period of *15 years* (from 1950 to 1965), English was to continue to be used for all official purposes of the Union, inter-state correspondence, and high court/supreme court proceedings. (This was subsequently extended indefinitely by the *Official Languages Act of 1963*).
3. *International Numerals:* The international form of Indian numerals (1, 2, 3...) was adopted for official Union use rather than Devanagari numerals.
4. *Eighth Schedule:* Regional languages were recognized in the Eighth Schedule (originally 14 languages, now 22), granting each state the right to conduct administration in its regional language.

