= Module 6: Final Adoption, Enforcement & Historiographical Significance

== 6.1 The Enactment Milestones
The Constituent Assembly completed its colossal task after *11 sessions* spanning *165 days* over *2 years, 11 months, and 18 days*. 
- Out of 7,635 amendments tabled, 2,473 were debated and disposed of on the floor.
- On *26 November 1949*, the Constitution of India was formally passed and adopted, signed by *284 members* present in the Constitution Hall. This date is celebrated nationwide as *Constitution Day* (*Samvidhan Diwas*).
- On *24 January 1950*, the Assembly met for its final sitting. The members signed two copies of the Constitution (one in English and one in Hindi, beautifully calligraphed by *Prem Behari Narain Raizada* and illuminated by artists of Shantiniketan under *Nandalal Bose*). *Dr. Rajendra Prasad* was elected as the first President of India.
- On *26 January 1950*, the Constitution came into full legal effect, marking the birth of the *Republic of India*. This date was deliberately chosen to commemorate the historic *Purna Swaraj Day* of 26 January 1930.

== 6.2 Ambedkar's Final Warning: The Triad of Liberty, Equality & Fraternity
In his closing speech to the Assembly on *25 November 1949*, Dr. B.R. Ambedkar delivered a timeless warning regarding the preservation of constitutional democracy:
1. *Abandon Direct Action Methods:* Nationalists must abandon the grammar of anarchy—satyagraha, civil disobedience, and strikes—now that constitutional channels for redress were open.
2. *Beware of Hero-Worship (Bhakti):* Quoting John Stuart Mill, he warned against laying personal liberties at the feet of great men: *"In politics, Bhakti or hero-worship is a sure road to degradation and to eventual dictatorship."*
3. *Social Democracy is Essential:* Political democracy cannot survive without social democracy: *"We must make our political democracy a social democracy as well. Political democracy cannot last unless there lies at the base of it social democracy."*

